Chapter 35: The Rust Market Exchange
Sector 7’s industrial lower ring, locally known as the Rust District, was a sprawling maze of rusted iron walkways, hiss-venting steam pipes, and neon-lit alleys where municipal surveillance drones rarely flew.
On Sunday morning, while Renna and the rest of Squad 4 were utilizing their newly funded Vanguard Guild training passes at the upper facilities, Kaelen took his approved weekend pass under the pretense of shopping for cheap scrap wires and replacement caddy straps.
Dressed in a heavy, faded dark coat with his hood pulled low over his brow, Kaelen slouched through the crowded, smog-choked bazaar. Beneath his baggy attire, his Level 14 frame—boasting 101 Strength and 102 Agility—operated with absolute, silent grace, stepping effortlessly through the churning crowd without a single person touching his coat.
He slipped down a narrow alley behind a flooded pump station, stepping into the damp shadow of a dead-end wall.
Kaelen engaged his passive [Shadow-Veil Cloak]. Ambient shadows clung to his frame, blurring his silhouette, while his newly acquired [Abyssal Shell Resonance] suppressed the vibration of his footsteps completely.
He tapped a hidden iron plate near the base of an old drainage pipe.
CLACK-CLICK.
A section of the concrete wall slid open, revealing a dimly lit spiral staircase descending deep into the city’s unmapped sub-foundations: The Rust Vault—the largest underground black market in the lower sectors.
The air inside the Rust Vault was thick with the scent of roasted meat, cheap synthetic tobacco, and unpurified monster mana. Dozens of shadowy figures—rogue mercenaries, unregistered salvagers, and off-duty guild agents wearing cloaks—navigated stalls selling everything from smuggled military stims to cursed monster bone daggers.
Kaelen navigated the chaotic floor with calm precision, his [Low-Light Spatial Perception] filtering out the neon glare and identifying the highest-security stall at the back of the bazaar.
Above the heavy iron door hung a rusted sign featuring a crossed wrench and anvil.
Kaelen pushed open the reinforced door. A heavy brass bell chimed softly.
Behind a counter of multi-layered bulletproof glass sat an old man with a mechanical brass eye, a thick grey beard, and a half-cyborg right arm covered in welding scars. He was smoking a dark cigar, carefully examining an obsidian core with a magnifying lens.
This was Old Fixer Gideon—a legendary retired black-market broker who had spent thirty years laundering illegal dungeon loot for unregistered hunters without ever turning over a name to municipal authorities.
"We're closed for private appraisals, kid," Gideon grunted without looking up from his lens, his mechanical eye zooming in with a faint whirrr. "Come back when you've got something better than rusted iron pipe."
Kaelen didn't stammer or slouch. His weak, timid demeanor vanished entirely.
He walked up to the counter, reached into his coat, and pulled out a heavy velvet pouch from his spatial salvage inventory.
He unceremoniously overturned the pouch onto the brass tray beneath the bulletproof glass.
CLINK-CLINK-CLINK-THUD!
The heavy tray illuminated in a brilliant, terrifying glow of deep crimson, electric blue, and dark void purple.
Eight Tier-C High-Density Mineral Cores, two Tier-C Behemoth Cores, a pristine Tier-B Sub-Trench Leviathan Core, and a pulsing, pristine Tier-B Elite Void-Scale Hydra Core rolled across the brass plate, spilling out alongside twelve pristine Void-Scale plates and sixteen titanium fangs.
THUD.
Gideon’s cigar fell straight out of his mouth, landing on his desk.
His mechanical eye spun frantically, its lens clicking and expanding as it attempted to register the sheer concentration of high-tier energy sitting on his tray.
"What in the hell..." Gideon gasped, his raspy voice cracking as he slammed his hands on the desk and stood up, staring at the glowing heap of high-tier loot in utter shock.
A single Tier-B Elite core from a Void-Scale Hydra was worth enough to buy an entire residential floor in Sector 3. It was the kind of drop that required an eighteen-man Tier-A Guild raiding party to harvest.
Gideon looked up from the glowing cores, his sharp, natural eye locking onto Kaelen’s shadowed face beneath the hood.
"You're a freshman..." Gideon whispered, his mechanical eye scanning Kaelen's worn coat, recognizing the faint scent of Vanguard Guild training grease lingering on his sleeves. "No... no freshman team clears a Tier-B Elite Hydra without the entire city's siren grid going off. Where did you pull this from?"
Kaelen met the old broker’s gaze with dark, cold eyes that held zero fear.
"Do you want to ask questions, Master Gideon, or do you want to take your fifteen percent laundering cut?" Kaelen’s voice was low, flat, and completely devoid of his public meekness.
Gideon stared at him for three long seconds. The old broker let out a dry, raspy laugh, picking up his cigar and shoving it back between his teeth.
"Fair enough, kid. Fair enough," Gideon muttered, his mechanical arm moving with practiced speed as he pulled out a secure, heavy metallic ledger and a high-grade digital credit transfer plate. "Rules of the Rust Vault: no names, no origins, only clean transactions."
Gideon swept the cores onto a digital scale, tapping his console as the numbers calculated the raw market value.
[Black Market Valuation Calculated]
 * 8x Tier-C Mineral Cores: 80,000 Credits
 * 2x Tier-C Behemoth Cores: 60,000 Credits
 * 1x Tier-B Leviathan Core: 250,000 Credits
 * 1x Tier-B Elite Void-Scale Hydra Core: 600,000 Credits
 * Rare Crafting Materials (Plates/Fangs): 150,000 Credits
[Total Gross Value:] 1,140,000 Credits
[Broker Cut (15%):] -171,000 Credits
[Net Payout:] 969,000 Credits
Gideon slid a black, untraceable corporate credit token across the tray alongside an encrypted digital keycard.
"Nine hundred and sixty-nine thousand clean, unregistered credits," Gideon said, his tone carrying a new level of deep, genuine respect. "No tax, no municipal tracking, backed directly by the Black-Market Reserve."
Kaelen picked up the heavy black credit token, slipping it into his coat pocket without a word.
"And the keycard?" Kaelen asked.
Gideon pointed his mechanical finger toward a reinforced steel blast door in the back of his shop.
"Access to my private, high-density Void-Forge Facility downstairs," Gideon replied, puffing a cloud of blue smoke. "You kept a few of those Void-Scale plates and Hydra fangs for yourself, didn't you? If you want to melt high-tier materials without Guild scanners registering the thermal flare, my forge is shielded with three feet of lead and anti-mana alloy. Fifty thousand credits per hour."
Kaelen tapped the keycard against his palm, a cold, satisfied smile touching his lips.
"Deduct the forge time from my balance," Kaelen said smoothly, turning toward the heavy blast door. "I'll be down there for two hours."
Gideon watched the hooded teenager walk through the door, his mechanical eye whirring as he took a deep drag from his cigar.
"A young boy on the outside... and a bloody monster on the inside," the old broker muttered to himself with a grin. "The Upper Sectors have no idea what's coming out of the slums."
